# THE GUARD REVISION THIS REPOSITORY AUDITED, BY CONTENT.
#
# .github/workflows/release.yml pins the shared release guards by commit
# SHA. A SHA is content-addressed and therefore immutable, so the pin
# cannot be edited under us. What a SHA does NOT do is tell a reader what
# is in it, and nothing states which BYTES were looked at before pinning
# them. That is what this file is for.
#
# Every file the guards execute is recorded below by SHA-256, taken at
# the pinned revision. tests/test_guard_pins.sh re-fetches each one and
# refuses any difference, so:
#
#   * bumping the pin without re-auditing goes RED, because the digests
#     below describe a revision the workflow no longer names;
#   * a pin that resolves to bytes other than the audited ones goes RED,
#     which is the falsifiable form of "we ran what we pinned";
#   * the check runs offline of any claim the guards make about
#     themselves, before a tag is ever pushed.
#
# WHAT REMAINS TRUSTED, stated plainly rather than papered over: that
# GitHub runs the workflow file living at the pinned SHA. A workflow
# cannot establish its own identity from inside itself, because a
# substituted file could simply print the expected answer. That is the
# platform's guarantee and the reason `uses:@<sha>` is written by SHA.
# Everything downstream of it is checked here by content.
#
# THE DIGESTS BELOW WERE WRITTEN BY `bash fleet/guard_pins.sh`, which
# fetched each file at 2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55 and hashed it. Do not
# hand-edit them, and do not copy this file from another repository: a
# copied record passes every check while having audited nothing, because
# the digests are genuinely the pinned revision's bytes. What a copy
# cannot carry is the reading.
#
# TO RE-AUDIT AFTER A PIN BUMP: read the diff, then re-run
# `bash fleet/guard_pins.sh <this repository>` and rewrite the note below.
#
#   https://github.com/nelsonduarte/capa-language/compare/<old>...2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUDIT NOTE, 2026-07-22, first adoption of the guards in this
# repository. There is NO PREVIOUS PIN here, so there is no diff to
# read; what follows is what was read at
# 2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55.
#
# ALL FIVE FILES BELOW WERE OPENED AND READ IN FULL at that revision,
# fetched from the API rather than from a sibling repository, and each
# one's digest recomputed locally and compared to the line below it.
# 1171 lines in total. None of them was accepted on its digest alone.
#
# What matters for THIS package, in the order it runs:
#
#   tools/capa_floor.sh (80 lines) reads `capa` from the [package] table
#     of capa.toml with a table-scoped awk (a bare grep would also match
#     a dependency's pin) and accepts only "X.Y.Z" or ">=X.Y.Z". It
#     therefore selects 1.18.1 here, which is the release measured
#     against this tree as the first one that completes the whole flow.
#     Every other form is refused rather than approximated. ONE STALE
#     COMMENT worth recording rather than omitting: its header says
#     "nothing in the compiler ENFORCES this field today ... this guard
#     is the only thing that tests the claim". That was true when it was
#     written and is no longer, since 1.19.0 reads dependency floors.
#     It is a comment; the code is unaffected and this pin is unchanged
#     by it.
#
#   tools/clean_room_build.sh (223 lines) is the one that decides what
#     "it builds" means for this package. It refuses a relative room, a
#     non-empty room, a tarball that extracts to anything but exactly
#     one top-level directory, an empty --run, and zero --run commands.
#     It unsets CAPA_PATH before running anything, which is the line
#     that stops the room reaching back into a development tree. Each
#     step's status is read explicitly with `set -e` off, so a failing
#     step cannot be reported as exit 0. The seven consumer commands in
#     release.yml are what it will execute, in order, inside the
#     extracted capa_validate-<tag>/.
#
#   tools/check_tag_version.sh (112 lines) compares the tag to
#     [package].version with the same table-scoped read, refuses a table
#     name that is not a plain identifier before interpolating it into a
#     regex, and refuses zero or more than one version line. Against the
#     0.2.0 set in capa.toml it will require the tag v0.2.0.
#
#   tools/verify_guard_digests.sh (148 lines) checks the digests
#     release.yml passes against the fetched guard tree, rejects a
#     digest path containing `..` or a leading `/`, and treats an empty
#     digests file as an error rather than a pass.
#
#   .github/workflows/release-guards.yml (608 lines) wires those four.
#     Confirmed by reading rather than assumed: every input reaches the
#     shell through `env:` and never by `${{ }}` interpolation into a
#     script body; `permissions: {}` at workflow level with `contents:
#     read` per job and no `id-token` anywhere; `job.workflow_sha` and
#     `job.workflow_repository` are both checked non-empty BEFORE the
#     checkout, because an empty ref makes actions/checkout fall back to
#     the default branch; the downloaded compiler is checked with
#     `gh attestation verify` and then with `capa --version`; the clean
#     room's tarball digest is exported as `tarball-sha256`, which is
#     the value release.yml and guard-selftest.yml both compare against.
#
# WHAT THIS RECORD STILL DOES NOT ESTABLISH: that GitHub runs the file
# living at the pinned SHA. That is the platform's guarantee and the
# reason `uses:@<sha>` is written by SHA; nothing running inside the
# guards can establish it, and this note does not pretend to.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

